1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,850
Hello everyone, and welcome back. We
are QAICON Lab, the Quantum and AI for

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:09,175
Computing, Optimization, and
Networking Lab, at Western University.

3
00:00:09,567 --> 00:00:14,139
<font color="#7FD4E6">And today, we find out what a code can
actually see when something goes wrong.</font>

4
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:19,909
At the end of the last section we
flipped the first qubit of the bit

5
00:00:19,909 --> 00:00:23,393
flip code, and the state moved out to
a box where the first check answers

6
00:00:23,393 --> 00:00:26,442
minus one, so we knew straight away
that something had happened.

7
00:00:27,033 --> 00:00:29,616
Now let's hit the same
qubit with a different

8
00:00:29,616 --> 00:00:32,728
error, a Z instead of an X,
and ask both checks again.

9
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:37,872
This time both checks answer plus one,
exactly as if nothing had happened at all.

10
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:42,406
<font color="#7FD4E6">Hmm, so a Z on one qubit just
doesn't bother this code?</font>

11
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:48,261
Well... it does bother it, quite a
lot. If you look at the encoded state,

12
00:00:48,261 --> 00:00:52,956
zero zero zero plus one one one has
turned into zero zero zero minus one

13
00:00:52,957 --> 00:00:57,586
one one, which is a different state,
and not one of the checks noticed.

14
00:00:58,167 --> 00:01:01,311
So the checks never look at
the error itself, they only

15
00:01:01,311 --> 00:01:04,571
learn something about it, and
the question for us is what

16
00:01:04,570 --> 00:01:07,714
exactly they learn, and what
can slip past them unseen.

17
00:01:08,633 --> 00:01:12,395
Let's start with a state in the code
and a single check, which the state

18
00:01:12,395 --> 00:01:16,105
answers with plus one, so applying
the check just gives the state back.

19
00:01:16,700 --> 00:01:21,212
Now suppose an error E hits first. If E
commutes with the check, you can slide

20
00:01:21,212 --> 00:01:25,431
the check straight past it, and the
damaged state still answers plus one.

21
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,438
But if E anticommutes with the check,
sliding past it throws off a minus sign,

22
00:01:30,438 --> 00:01:34,992
like the ones we counted last time, and
now the damaged state answers minus one.

23
00:01:35,433 --> 00:01:37,517
<font color="#7FD4E6">Every time? It
isn't random?</font>

24
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:42,217
Not random at all, no. The answer depends
only on the error and the check, and not

25
00:01:42,217 --> 00:01:46,406
on which encoded state you started from,
which is why we can measure the checks

26
00:01:46,405 --> 00:01:49,681
without learning, or disturbing, the
information we've stored.

27
00:01:50,267 --> 00:01:53,494
So if you write a one for every
check that answers minus one,

28
00:01:53,494 --> 00:01:56,613
and a zero for every check that
answers plus one, you get a

29
00:01:56,613 --> 00:01:59,302
short string of bits, which is
called the syndrome.

30
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:03,716
For the bit flip code, the three
single flips give three different

31
00:02:03,716 --> 00:02:07,532
syndromes, one zero, one one, and
zero one, and that's exactly why

32
00:02:07,533 --> 00:02:10,116
the three dimmed boxes could tell
them apart.

33
00:02:11,033 --> 00:02:14,244
Now, here is one for you
to try. If qubits one and

34
00:02:14,244 --> 00:02:17,323
two both flip at once,
what does each check say?

35
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:21,522
<font color="#7FD4E6">The first check sees
two flips, so they</font>

36
00:02:21,522 --> 00:02:25,022
<font color="#7FD4E6">cancel. And the second
sees one, so zero one?</font>

37
00:02:25,367 --> 00:02:30,447
Spot on. Each check just counts the minus
signs it gets from both errors together,

38
00:02:30,447 --> 00:02:34,899
so syndromes add up, with two ones making
a zero, the way a parity does.

39
00:02:35,467 --> 00:02:40,143
But zero one is also the syndrome of a
single flip on qubit three, so a decoder

40
00:02:40,143 --> 00:02:44,939
that trusts it will flip qubit three, and
now all three qubits have been flipped.

41
00:02:46,033 --> 00:02:50,007
<font color="#7FD4E6">Wait, so it lands on one one
one? That's the other code word.</font>

42
00:02:50,333 --> 00:02:54,809
Right. X on all three qubits turns
zero zero zero into one one one and

43
00:02:54,809 --> 00:02:59,285
back again, it passes both checks,
and it isn't a check itself, so the

44
00:02:59,284 --> 00:03:03,111
code has quietly been turned into a
different encoded state.

45
00:03:04,633 --> 00:03:08,650
So every Pauli error you could name
lands in one of three regions, depending

46
00:03:08,650 --> 00:03:12,506
on what the checks say about it, and the
innermost region is the easiest.

47
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:16,110
If the error is one of the
checks, or a product of them,

48
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:19,668
like Z Z on the first two qubits,
then it fixes every state in the

49
00:03:19,668 --> 00:03:22,952
code, so it did nothing at all
and there's nothing to repair.

50
00:03:23,533 --> 00:03:26,604
If the error anticommutes
with at least one check, the

51
00:03:26,604 --> 00:03:29,965
syndrome isn't all zeros, and
we say the error is detected,

52
00:03:29,966 --> 00:03:32,921
which covers every single
flip in the bit flip code.

53
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:38,145
And then there is this region in between,
the errors that commute with every check

54
00:03:38,145 --> 00:03:42,675
without being checks themselves, like the
Z on qubit one, or the X on all three.

55
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:46,765
<font color="#7FD4E6">So those are the ones that change
the state and nobody notices.</font>

56
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:50,263
Exactly. An error in there
passes every test and still

57
00:03:50,263 --> 00:03:53,904
changes the encoded state, so
it's called a logical error, and

58
00:03:53,904 --> 00:03:57,485
the strings that live there are
called the logical operators.

59
00:03:58,067 --> 00:04:01,140
I like that the same strings
play both parts, because a

60
00:04:01,140 --> 00:04:04,498
logical operator applied by
accident is a failure, while the

61
00:04:04,498 --> 00:04:08,084
same operator applied on purpose
is a gate on the encoded qubit.

62
00:04:09,633 --> 00:04:12,669
So how strong a code is comes
down to how short its shortest

63
00:04:12,669 --> 00:04:15,962
logical error is, and we measure
a string by counting the letters

64
00:04:15,962 --> 00:04:18,947
in it that aren't the identity,
which is called its weight.

65
00:04:19,533 --> 00:04:23,104
The smallest weight of any logical
error is the distance of the code,

66
00:04:23,104 --> 00:04:26,675
and an error lighter than that can
never be a logical error, so it is

67
00:04:26,675 --> 00:04:29,301
either caught by the checks or
does nothing at all.

68
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:32,790
So for the bit flip code, what
do you think the distance is?

69
00:04:33,967 --> 00:04:37,494
<font color="#7FD4E6">X X X is the logical
error, so three?</font>

70
00:04:37,833 --> 00:04:41,819
Well, that's the natural guess,
because it's the logical error we just

71
00:04:41,819 --> 00:04:46,094
met. But remember the Z on qubit one
from the start, which sits in the same

72
00:04:46,094 --> 00:04:50,080
region with a weight of only one, so
the distance of this code is one.

73
00:04:50,667 --> 00:04:53,926
And that matches what you saw in
lecture seven, where this code

74
00:04:53,926 --> 00:04:57,501
could fix any one bit flip but not
a general error on a single qubit.

75
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:02,087
In general, a set of errors can
all be corrected as long as no

76
00:05:02,087 --> 00:05:06,401
two of them multiply to a logical
error, and that is just what went

77
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:10,518
wrong before, since X one X two
times X three is X on all three.

78
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:14,672
So with distance d, any two
errors of weight at most d minus

79
00:05:14,672 --> 00:05:18,062
one over two, rounded down,
multiply to something lighter

80
00:05:18,061 --> 00:05:21,633
than d, and a code of distance d
corrects every one of them.

81
00:05:23,167 --> 00:05:26,347
So the checks never see an
error itself, only the pattern

82
00:05:26,347 --> 00:05:29,641
of which checks it anticommutes
with, and a code is only as

83
00:05:29,641 --> 00:05:32,707
strong as the lightest error
that none of them can see.

84
00:05:33,133 --> 00:05:36,911
<font color="#7FD4E6">Then can we build a code where every
single-qubit error gets caught?</font>

85
00:05:37,233 --> 00:05:41,656
We can, and in section eight point three
we'll meet three of them, each with

86
00:05:41,656 --> 00:05:46,315
distance three, so any error on a single
qubit is not only caught but corrected.
